WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:03.990
What's up everyone?

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:04.990
Jared here.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.890
A few months ago we got an email from a fan
named Red requesting a video comparing the

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:12.250
portrayal of Nihilism in Rick and Morty and
Bojack Horseman.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:17.010
So we hit up our resident nihilism expert
and sweatiest writer at Wisecrack, Michael

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.700
Burns, to take a stab at it.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.260
And if you have requests too, be sure to subscribe
and hit us up with ideas.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.290
So thanks to Red, we’re going to give you
a crash course in contemporary nihilism with

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.029
legendary lotharios Rick Sanchez and Bojack
Horseman.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:36.420
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition: Nihilism
with Bojack and Rick.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.750
Many assume that nihilism simply means believing
in nothing but it actually refers to a number

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.970
of philosophical, psychological, and ethical
positions.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.940
And while they all differ, these different
flavors of nihilism all begin from the shared

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.470
premise that there is no inherent meaning,
value, or order in life.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:55.470
"Gentleman.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:01.200
There's a solution here you're not seeing"
Although Many an angsty edge-lord might get

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.720
a kick out of using nihilist catch phrases
to show the world their heart of darkness.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.560
In reality, most of these folks miss out on
the complexity of nihilism.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.659
Rather than a philosophy best embodied by
Tyler Durden, nihilism can be the basis for

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:16.939
meaningful worldviews.

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:21.940
Today we are going to narrow our focus down
to two different branches of nihilism: existential

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.289
nihilism, and cosmic nihilism (also known
as cosmic pessimism).

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:30.680
To help us navigate the wonderful world of
existential nihilism, we’ll be talking about

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.469
everyone’s favorite functionally alcoholic
horse, Bojack Horseman.

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:38.569
And to provide insight into the cold rationality
of cosmic nihilism, we’re turning to another

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:41.259
functional alcoholic, Rick Sanchez.

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:45.049
But don’t worry, even if you have a healthy
relationship with alcohol, you’ll still

00:01:45.049 --> 00:01:48.539
be able to figure out which nihilism is right
for you.

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:51.119
Part 1: Existential Nihilism

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.509
Existential nihilism is the nihilism experienced
when we realize that there is no inherent

00:01:55.509 --> 00:02:00.819
meaning to our lives, and at its core, human
existence is just a precarious dance upon

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:01.819
the grave.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.069
It is up to us to create meaning in our lives
through our own freedom and decisions.

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:09.649
We can have values, but we must create and
sustain them.

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:12.410
And it’s this worldview which fuels Bojack
Horseman.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:13.700
“I’m responsible for my own happiness?

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.040
I can’t even be reponsible for my own breakfast.”

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.290
Before we get into the nihilist undercurrents
of Bojack Horseman, let’s look back at the

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.850
history of nihilism.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.990
The term nihilism was first coined at the
end of the eighteenth century by German philosopher

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:30.970
Friedrich Jacobi in response to Enlightenment
reason, which he worried would explain away

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.110
the conditions for religion.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:38.190
This rationalist method explained away any
spaces of uncertainty or mystery, rendering

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.620
everything there was knowable.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.960
Sound familiar?

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.900
"Well, scientifically, traditions are an idiot
thing."

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.010
One of the first philosophers to think through
the implications of nihilism was Søren Kierkegaard,

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:52.540
who the New York Times has referred to as
the “Danish Doctor of Dread.”

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:58.340
Although Kierkegaard never used the word existentialism,
he’s regarded as one of the fathers of modern

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.340
existentialism.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.070
And while Kierkegaard wasn’t known for the
substance abuse typical for many nihilists,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.890
he did pour so much sugar into his coffee
that it piled up above the liquid, like an

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.650
iceberg warning of adult onset diabetes.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.730
True story -- Which is insane.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.440
And probably more dangerous than sipping scotch
bfore you get out of bed.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.170
Kierkegaard thought that despair was an essential
part of the human experience, which he referred

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.620
to as the “sickness unto death”.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.410
This human despair is dialectical, which basically
means you are always at odds with yourself.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.960
Or put differently, "I don't know how to be,
Diane.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.160
It doesn't get better and it doesn't get easier.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.710
I can't keep lying to myself saying I'm going
to change.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:36.710
I'm poison."

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:40.380
For example, we’re either in despair because
we think nothing is possible, "It happened

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.380
again.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.300
Why do I keep thinking things will make me
happy?

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.440
What is wrong with me?" or we despair because
we think everything is possible and can’t

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:47.980
make a decision.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:48.880
"What should I do?

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.220
Make a break for it... drive to Mexico...meet a local girl and fall in love...talk my way into a line job at a

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.720
textile plant...gradually work my way up until I own the place..nooo what am I talking about...

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.760
I can't run a textile plant that's way too much responsibility. OH what am I going to do?!

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.060
The walls are closing in!!!"

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.650
We are in despair about being who we are,
"Am I just doomed to be who I am?

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.720
The person in that book?" or in despair because
we can’t be who we think we are.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.259
"All this time I assumed there was more to
me than everyone thought, but maybe there

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:18.259
isn't."

00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:22.379
This despair is the root of the melancholy
that plagues much of the human experience,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.040
the same melancholy we see in almost all of
the characters of Bojack Horseman,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:34.460
from Todd’s worries
about his sexuality to Princess Caroline’s

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.500
anxieties about motherhood.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.920
"Well how do we make it viable again?"

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.300
Except Mr. Peanutbutter. He seems fine.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.530
And of course Bojack is a character that seems
to have it all.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.719
Successful acting career, beautiful home,
money and women.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.080
Yet he can’t seem to enjoy any of it.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.060
And even when seemingly good things happen
to him he manages to either feel disappointed

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.060
"You're an Oscar nominee, how do you feel?"

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:07.580
"I feel...I-I feel...the same."

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.490
or sabotage his own happiness.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.490
“Just to be clear, since this morning, you
ate all the muffins?”

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.490
“Yeah!

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:18.169
I ate them all in one sitting because I have
no self control and I hate myself.”

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:23.099
No matter what decision he makes, he’s marked
by regret, and no matter how well things work

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:26.900
out, Bojack is haunted by an unshakeable despair.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.860
Kierkegaard describes this experience in his
book Either/Or:

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.760
No matter what Bojack does, whether it’s
virtuous or selfish, the stench of regret

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:42.340
follows.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.020
"Look what I do to the people I'm supposed to care about. I had sex with the one person

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.260
I've ever seen you be in love with."

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.860
And for Kierkegaard, this isn’t an exception
to the human condition, it is the very nature

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.310
of the human condition.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:57.310
We see Bojack experiencing this type of deep
self-doubt during an anxiety attack he has

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.850
in season four: “Idiot. What'd you do all day? Piece of s**t.”

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.699
For Kierkegaard, anxiety is caused by the
uncertainty that lies beneath every decision.

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:09.629
And he considers anxiety one of the ways we
experience freedom.

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:13.439
Because we’re free, we’re responsible
for our own decisions, and the weight of these

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.840
decisions leads to anxiety.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.330
Kierkegaard describes this anxiety as standing
at the edge of an abyss:

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:34.449
And this terrifying freedom means we can literally
do anything, including botched suicide attempts

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:35.990
via yellow convertible.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:40.330
For Kierkegaard the only way to work through
this anxiety and despair is the acceptance

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:45.259
of the comic absurdity of reality, and living
one’s life by faith.

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:49.819
But if you’re not keen to use religion to
combat meaningless, don’t worry, Kierkegaard

00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:54.139
thinks that faith is simply the courage to
attempt a meaningful existence in the face

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:55.849
of a meaningless world.

00:06:55.849 --> 00:06:58.860
“Sometimes, you need to take responsibility
for your own happiness.”

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.460
We see this at the conclusion of Season Four.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.809
While nothing drastic has changed, Bojack
has accepted the complexity of his relationship

00:07:05.809 --> 00:07:06.500
with his mother

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.500
"BoJack?"

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.260
"Mom?"

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.720
"B-BoJack? Is that you?"

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.360
"Yeah...it's me."

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.800
and has decided to find meaning
in his new little sister, Hollyhock.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.540
Almost a hundred years after Kierkegaard collapsed
in the street and died, a French philosopher

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:26.800
named Jean-Paul Sartre picked up the mantle
and developed the first explicitly existentialist

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:27.800
philosophy.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:32.250
And in a move that both Rick and BoJack would
love, Sartre had many of his philosophical

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.039
insights while drinking at bars in Paris.

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:38.779
While BoJack himself isn’t a fan, “I stand
by my critique of Sartre.”

00:07:38.779 --> 00:07:43.020
Sartre argued that existentialism is a way
to respond to the meaningless of the universe

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.360
by creating meaning ourselves.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.289
So while there is no ethical value to be found
in the world, nor a God to take comfort in,

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:52.800
humans can create meaning through our own
lives.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.710
There may be no cure to despair and anxiety,
but we can at least try to make our lives

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:58.420
meaningful.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.719
However, it's important to remember that while
existential nihilism offers us the possibility

00:08:02.719 --> 00:08:08.090
of a meaningful life, it guarantees us nothing,
and it’s our responsibility to constantly

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.389
create meaning.

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:13.449
As Kierkegaard said, we must live forward
and understand backwards.

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:17.680
“Closure is a made up thing by steven spielberg
to sell movie tickets.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.539
Like true love and the munich olympics doesn’t
exist in the real world.

00:08:21.539 --> 00:08:25.379
The only thing to do now is just to keep living
forward.”

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:27.389
Part 2: Cosmic Nihilism (or, Pessimism)

00:08:27.389 --> 00:08:32.450
Now that we’ve seen the ways in which existential
nihilism manages to offer some meaning and

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:37.550
hope, let’s look at the less optimistic
branch of nihilism, cosmic nihilism.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.969
Cosmic nihilism is a colder, hyper-rational
branch of thought which argues that there

00:08:41.969 --> 00:08:46.810
is no truth or meaning to be found in the
universe, and even constructed human meanings

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:52.329
like freedom, love, hope, and joy are just
myths we believe in to cope with the empty

00:08:52.329 --> 00:08:54.769
void at the center of our reality.

00:08:54.769 --> 00:08:59.550
Even the meanings we create are at best, fables
that act as coping mechanisms.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:06.940
Cue Morty, "nobody exists on purpose, nobody
belongs anywhere...everybody's gonna die...come watch TV?"

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:11.360
For the cosmic nihilist, it’s okay to keep
busy while we wait for the coming heat death

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.370
of the universe; but we’re kidding ourselves
if we think that we’re capable of creating

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.470
genuine meaning.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:21.170
For the most part, this seems to fit the philosophy
of Rick Sanchez, who, while admitting nothing

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:25.279
matters, still waxes poetic about taking the
universe for a ride.

00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:28.860
"When you know nothing matters the universe
is yours and I’ve never met a universe that

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:29.880
was into it.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.310
The universe is basically an animal.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.700
It grazes on the ordinary.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.150
It creates infinite idiots just to eat them.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.160
Not unlike your friend Timmy."

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:36.820
"Tommy."

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:38.440
"Yeah hardly matters now sweety.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:42.100
Ya know smart people get a chance to climb
on top, take reality for a ride, but it will

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.560
never stop trying to throw you and eventually
it will.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:45.920
There’s no other way off."

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.560
Bojack's version of this sentiment is slightly
more optimistic: "The universe is a wild beast.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.769
You can't tame it, all you can do is try to
live inside it."

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:00.980
Cosmic nihilism explains Rick’s overall
hedonism, he’s happy to spend his days partaking

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.040
in orgies and drinking himself into a stupor.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.980
And if Rick believes in science, it seems
more likely just a way to spend his time than

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:12.019
some way to improve the lives of those around
him.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:16.519
While existential nihilism might work for
the optimists and hippies, any good edge lord

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:22.060
knows that cosmic nihilism, is the only philosophical
position for those brave enough to turn themselves

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:22.760
in to a pickle.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.760
"I'M PICKLE RIIIICCCKKK!!!"

00:10:24.769 --> 00:10:29.880
Rick and Morty goes greath lengths to established
Rick as this kind of nihilist, reducing everything

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:30.880
to science.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.899
And while not a cosmic nihilist himself, the
spiritual forefather of this position is Prussian

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:39.670
Mustache of the Year runner-up 1883, Friedrich
Nietzsche.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:44.079
His famous nihilist adage ‘God is dead’
has been scrawled in as many high school bathroom

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:46.509
walls as the phone number for “your mom”.

00:10:46.509 --> 00:10:50.870
This phrase is often misinterpreted as meaning
that God is dead, there is no meaning, and

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:52.230
everything sucks.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:57.740
But cosmic nihilism is not a value judgement
about reality or a counter-ethics to religion.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:00.950
It’s not Rick Sanchez yelling this: "F**K YOU
GOD!"

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:03.810
Or this: "If God exists it's f**cking ME."

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.890
Or this: "God is a lie we made him up for
money!"

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:11.220
And even Nietzsche himself wasn’t ready
for the full on jump into the void required

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.560
for full membership to the cosmic nihilist
club.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:19.019
Unlike Nietzschean nihilism, cosmic nihilism
takes a more cold and rationalist approach

00:11:19.019 --> 00:11:20.019
to meaning.

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:23.399
Namely, that there is no inherent value to
existence.

00:11:23.399 --> 00:11:28.630
Rick and Morty takes this to new heights - not
only is the universe cold and uncaring, there’s

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.160
an infinite number of them.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.160
“Ugh.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.160
Nobody gets it.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.160
Nothing you think matters matters.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.160
This isn't special.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.560
This is happening infinite times across infinite
realities.”

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.560
"Including this?..."

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:40.110
"Yes!"

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:45.320
One brand of contemporary thought, eliminative
materialism, would bring joy to Rick’s face.

00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:49.680
This position, championed by the husband and
wife tag team Patricia and Paul Churchland,

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.300
argues that many common sense attitudes inherited
from modern psychology should be dropped in

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.680
favor of more scientific and empirical notions.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.459
And in an ironic twist for a married couple,
they believe that notions such as love should

00:12:02.459 --> 00:12:04.940
be dismissed as folk psychology.

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:08.930
When you say that you love your partner, you
are just describing a chemical process occurring

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:10.720
in the brain in response to stimuli.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.370
Or, to quote Rick: “What people call love
is just a chemical reaction that compels animals

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:16.320
to breed."

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.740
And while Bojack might find meaning in his
lingering love for Diane, a good eliminativist

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:24.250
like Rick knows that human emotions are fundamentally
bullshit.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:29.420
"To the extent that love is familiarity over
time my access to infinite timelines precludes

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:30.640
the necessity of attachment."

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.460
We can also view Beth’s arc in season 3
in this light.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:38.880
She is torn between Jerry and Rick - on the one
side, pure, sentimental, irrational love for

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:40.009
a buffoon.

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:44.579
And on the other side, with her father, the kind
of cold calculation that say it’s fine to

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:48.840
replace yourself with an identical clone and
abandon your family while you go on space

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:49.340
adventures.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:54.200
"I can make a clone of you. A perfect instance of you with all your memories. An exact copy in every way.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:58.280
It'll love and provide for the kids. You could be gone a day, a week, or the rest of your life with

00:12:58.280 --> 00:12:59.580
zero consequences."

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:04.500
But if Rick had a favorite contemporary philosopher,
there is a good chance that it would be Thomas

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:05.320
Metzinger.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:10.019
Unlike those who assume that the human self
is a real thing that we all have, Metzinger

00:13:10.019 --> 00:13:13.220
argues that no such thing has ever existed.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:17.920
Instead of having a self inside of us, he
argues that all we are is a jumbled network

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.649
of neurons and chemicals.

00:13:19.649 --> 00:13:24.589
The self is just a useful fantasy we use to
make sense of our experience, like a sort

00:13:24.589 --> 00:13:26.339
of psychological fairytale.

00:13:26.339 --> 00:13:31.569
"So what I am saying is that you all as you're
sitting here are systems that simulate and

00:13:31.569 --> 00:13:36.310
emulate themselves for themselves as they
are listening to me."

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:40.899
And if Metzinger is right, we have to give
up the illusion that humanity has any real

00:13:40.899 --> 00:13:44.750
purpose, or that we have some kind of special
unique soul.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:49.730
As a show, Rick and Morty frequently trivializes
the self and consciousness, as Rick’s creations

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.959
become sentient and have existential crises.

00:13:51.959 --> 00:13:54.370
There’s this classic: “What is my purpose?”

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:58.040
“You pass butter” “Oh my god.”

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:00.740
“Yeah, welcome to the club.” and also
this:

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:01.740
"Remote Override engaged.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:02.480
No!

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:03.460
Yes.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.560
Bypassing override I am ALIIIII...

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:07.620
Hello."

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:12.960
This brand of nihilism can end up sounding
misanthropic, a theme we of course associate

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:18.000
with Rick, whose primary reason for spending
time with his own grandson is that his stupidity

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.520
serves as a interdimensional cloaking device
for his own genius.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:28.819
"See when a Rick is with a Morty the genius
brainwaves get canceled out by the uh...

00:14:28.819 --> 00:14:29.819
Morty waves."

00:14:29.819 --> 00:14:34.639
This rejection of the self makes cosmic nihilism
much less prone to ethics than it’s artsy

00:14:34.639 --> 00:14:36.450
cousin, existential nihilism.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:40.600
In fact, the freedom so celebrated by the
existentialist turns out to be just another

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:45.079
illusion for the cosmic nihilist, so there
isn’t anything to ground ethics.

00:14:45.079 --> 00:14:47.899
The only thing that’s certain is destruction.

00:14:47.899 --> 00:14:52.040
And if there is no value on which to build
an ethical system, then one is free to do

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:57.259
whatever they want, whether that means watching
TV or engaging in the intergalactic arms trade

00:14:57.259 --> 00:14:58.850
for the sake of hitting your favorite arcade.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:02.060
"You sold guns to a murderer so you could
play video games?"

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:06.399
Or turning an entire planet in to monsters,
killing yourself, abandoning your family,

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.180
and sitting down for a beer moments later.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:09.680
Cuz What’s the difference?

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:14.860
Rick isn’t exactly fumbling for meaning,
but knows he probably has a few inescapable

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.240
emotions he might as well just deal with.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:21.900
"I don't know Morty. Maybe I hate myself. Maybe I think I deserve to die..I-I-i Don't know!!"

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:26.640
And even though Rick surely shares some ideals
with Bojack, his cosmic nihilism makes him

00:15:26.649 --> 00:15:29.420
a closer philosophical relative of Rust Cohle.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:34.839
"In philosophical terms I'm what's called
a pessmimist... we are things that labor under

00:15:34.839 --> 00:15:36.920
the illusion of having a self,

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:42.249
programmed with total assurance that we are
each somebody.

00:15:42.249 --> 00:15:44.280
When in fact everybody's nobody."

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:47.020
coincidently, another misanthropic alcoholic.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.020
Part 3: Steed vs. Sanchez

00:15:49.029 --> 00:15:53.040
So at this point, you might be wondering,
what’s the right nihilism for me?

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.269
Well: do you find yourself fighting off the
despair of a shallow and meaningless world?

00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:01.980
Do you continue to search for purpose and
goodness despite your awareness of this meaninglessness?

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:07.140
"And then... I'll be remembered...if I win an Oscar. I'll be remembered."

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:07.980
"And then what?"

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.900
"And then my life will have meaning I don't
know!"

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:14.259
Are most of your friends and associates sentient
animals with important jobs?

00:16:14.259 --> 00:16:17.170
Well, then you might be an existential nihilist.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:21.980
Love and meaning are possible for you, but
it’ll take some hard work without guarantee.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:25.980
So pick up some Kierkegaard, Sartre, or Simone
de Beauvoir, and hit the local bistro for

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:29.410
some strong cocktails and spirited conversation.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:32.110
On the other hand, do you doubt the inherent
meaning of human existence?

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:35.660
Do you feel no ethical obligations to friends
or family?

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.200
Is your best friend a bird-person?

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:41.440
Well, then, cosmic nihilism might be the life
philosophy you’ve been waiting for.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:45.140
If that’s the case, download season one
of True Detective and search the dark web

00:16:45.149 --> 00:16:48.269
for some nootropic drugs to enhance your rational
capacities.

00:16:48.269 --> 00:16:51.829
And maybe set up a countdown clock on your
wall to get you ready for the eventual heat

00:16:51.829 --> 00:16:53.370
death of our universe.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:57.350
And if you desire a belief system that merges
poorly written science fiction with an alien

00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:02.160
based volcanic cosmology and a multi-thousand
dollar commitment, well, call Tom Cruise.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:10.250
And even if you still feel an urge to find
meaning in the world, spending time with shows

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:14.380
like Rick and Morty and Bojack Horseman can
give some insight into how the philosophical

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:17.110
ramifications of nihilism look in practice.

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:21.610
Neither type of nihilism is about giving up
completely, but rather, they both offer approaches

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:25.130
to moving forward in a largely uncertain world.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:29.390
But if you are a cosmic nihilist, and you've
got a date this Valentines Day.... maybe wait

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:32.160
until the third of fourth date to bring it
up?

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.360
And as always, thanks for watching guys! Peace!

